WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:11.010
Seen from afar, the city of Ordos, in China’s
Inner Mongolia, isn’t all that remarkable.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:17.670
A bit artificial-looking, sure — with its
blue Mongolian yurt-inspired homes protruding

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.900
from the desert.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.960
Some might liken its Genghis Khan Mausoleum,
its public light show, and giant prancing

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.830
horse statues to Disneyland.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.250
It seems designed more to be looked at than
lived in.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:38.710
As you move closer, however, the moment buildings
become more than mere shapes, you will undoubtedly

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.660
feel a certain eeriness consume you.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.100
Something seems… off.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.180
Your eyes feel lied to.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.630
Because no amount of elaborate museums, extravagant
stadiums, malls, or even horse statues can

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.630
make up for what Ordos is missing: People!

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:04.210
Now, in fairness, it’s not, strictly speaking,
empty.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:10.079
Low-density planning may conceal them, but
a few people do work, study, shop, and live

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:15.149
here — about 150,000, according to the government.

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:20.619
But nowhere near the one million it originally
planned for in the early 2000s.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:27.240
Then, newly rich after 1/3rd of the country’s
natural gas and 1/6th of its coal reserves

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.490
were found here, local officials had grand
ambitions to build a newer, modern, showpiece

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.490
district.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:41.389
Here, it built all the best schools, hospitals,
and, yes, Genghis Khan mausoleums.

00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:46.700
What it forgot were supermarkets, department
stores, and bars.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:53.569
And so, for its first few years, just 30,000
people lived in a city designed for 30 times

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:55.079
more.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:02:00.840
Ordos is far from the crowded, vibrant, modern
China you’ve come to expect.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.560
Which makes it a perfect Rorschach test.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.580
When news of this weird “ghost city” first
emerged in 2011, provocative photos of empty

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:16.310
and half-finished buildings fit all too neatly
into the story of China the world was eager

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.860
to hear.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:24.980
The picture was one of comic-book-level, growth-at-all-costs,
bridge-to-nowhere inefficiencies.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:31.940
It seemed to validate the most extreme skeptics
— that the whole of China, 1.4 billion people

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:38.350
and all, might one day be revealed as one
giant, elaborate cardboard façade.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:42.920
Others argued this was, most charitably, a
misunderstanding!

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.571
As so often happens with this opaque country,
the language barrier, a lack of context, or

00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:53.640
just plain sensationalism conspired to tell
the wrong story.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:59.580
China plans years ahead, they said, and this
was just an example of especially early planning

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.040
for millions of incoming migrants.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.320
In hindsight, both reactions contain some
truth.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.370
Ordos does have an interesting story to tell.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.470
Just, not the one you might expect.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:19.330
The missing context, at the time, was far
stranger than what the unimaginative pessimists

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.210
concocted:

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:26.890
Nearly all of these half-finished homes have
owners — the vast majority of which have

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.430
no intention of ever moving in.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.810
Ordos is not unique.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:39.260
All over China are millions of empty, some
unfinished, but almost universally sold homes

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:46.400
— not just in far-flung corners but also
in Beijing, Shanghai, and Shenzhen.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.740
Over one-fifth of all urban homes — 65 million
in all — sit vacant.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:58.260
Housing is the second of China’s chronically-ill
internal organs — the symptoms of which

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:04.030
it feels quite acutely in the form of impossibly
unaffordable real estate.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:09.350
Acknowledging the severity of the problem,
however, admits of weakness, suggests poor

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:15.209
governance, and stands in stark contrast to
the mighty, robust, superpower image China

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:17.359
is desperate to project.

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:24.150
It’s time to look past China’s bold, aggressive
exterior, peak inside at the true state of

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.879
its health, and make a diagnosis.

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:29.699
