Pops”, or one of thousands of other great

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:38.599
audiobooks for free with the link in the description.

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:46.229
Anyone who's taken Macroeconomics 101 is familiar
with the letters C, I, G, and NX.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:52.900
* C is Consumption — the phones, toys, and
macroeconomics textbooks we don’t illegally

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.900
download.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:05:00.060
* Investment is anything bought but not consumed
— think factories, machines, and homes.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.680
* G stands for government spending,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.300
* And finally, Net Exports are what is sold
overseas.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.439
Add these handy little numbers up and you
have a country’s GDP.

00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:16.009
The great thing about this formula is that
it turns abstract terms like “growth”

00:05:16.009 --> 00:05:18.460
into concrete buckets.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:24.009
What it means that there are really only four
ways for a country to grow its economy.

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:29.590
And while a dollar is a dollar, regardless
of which bucket it comes from, not all buckets

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:33.680
are created equal — something China knows
well.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.050
Its early growth famously relied on exports.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:42.550
It became the “world’s factory” and
gained a reputation for cheap, mass production.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.830
The problem is that exports are a double-edged
sword.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:52.030
They rely on a surplus of cheap labor, which
means, by definition, wages are low.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.810
You can only compete with the entire rest
of the world for so long — and neither do

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.370
you want to.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:05.680
Low-value manufacturing has long since moved
South, to places like Vietnam, Laos, and Bangladesh.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:12.589
Next, came investment, which peaked at nearly
half of China’s GDP in 2011, and is reflected

00:06:12.589 --> 00:06:16.809
in its shiny new airports, train stations,
and office buildings.

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:21.499
But there are natural limits on investment,
too.

00:06:21.499 --> 00:06:27.570
In the beginning, basic infrastructure like
roads, bridges, and ports see quick and substantial

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.999
returns.

00:06:28.999 --> 00:06:30.690
These are the easy targets.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:37.060
Eventually, a nation simply runs out of them,
forcing it to take on riskier and less profitable

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.870
projects.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:44.009
Government spending, meanwhile, is constrained
by tax revenues and debt.

00:06:44.009 --> 00:06:46.979
That leaves only: C — Consumption.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:52.199
Consumption-led growth is the final stage
on the road to economic development.

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:57.160
It means workers are educated, productive,
and have enough confidence to let go of their

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.319
hard-earned cash.

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:05.360
If China is ever to become the rich nation
it dreams of, it will first need to stop saving

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.550
and get shopping.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.210
This may come as a surprise.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.710
We’ve all been bombarded with reminders
of China’s “rising middle class” and

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.940
immense purchasing power.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:21.569
But consumption is the product of population
size times individual spending.

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:26.749
When multiplied together, China, the country,
may be a force to be reckoned with — but,

00:07:26.749 --> 00:07:32.119
it’s important to remember, the former — its
massive population size — is doing most

00:07:32.119 --> 00:07:33.430
of the work.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:40.419
Individually, Chinese consumers really don’t
spend very much — just 32% of GDP — less

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:45.860
than half that of the US, and far below countries
like Japan and Germany.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:50.999
Worse, this number has actually been decreasing
over time.

00:07:50.999 --> 00:07:53.259
There are a few reasons why.

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:59.550
First, China has a weak social security system,
forcing workers to save for themselves and

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.889
spend with greater caution.

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:07.919
Second, income tax, in all its various forms,
can be as high as 40%.

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:15.089
But there’s another reason: Chinese consumers
are spending, but only on one thing, something

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:17.979
not considered ‘consumption’: houses!

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:24.159
Now, there is, of course, no one “Chinese
consumer” or “Chinese mind” any more

00:08:24.159 --> 00:08:28.289
than there is a singular “American” or
“European” one.

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:34.169
But that need not stop us from observing what
cannot adequately be described as a mere tendency

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:36.120
or trend:

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.950
China is absolutely obsessed with real estate.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:46.550
For starters, its homeownership rate is among
the highest in the world — 90% — to much

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.740
of the developed world’s mid-60s.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.330
It gets much weirder, still.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:09:00.100
If you can believe it, the majority of recent
purchases have been 2nd and 3rd homes.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:06.160
In 2018, for instance, 87% of new home buyers
already owned at least one.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.180
Needless to say, these numbers are… peculiar.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:15.580
If everyone owns two or three homes, who is
left to live in all of them?

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:18.740
The answer is absolutely no one.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.810
Vacancy rates vary considerably by city size,
but remain high, in absolute terms, across

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:25.940
the board.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:31.670
Once again, China ranks near the top in what
may look like a lazy repurposing of earlier

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:32.810
graphs.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.520
Clearly, all these homes are intended as investments.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:41.490
But that only answers one question by raising
another.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:43.600
Again, why?

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.160
China’s passion for real estate began in
the late 90s, when it teased privatization

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:54.400
by offering state employees the right to buy
their homes at a steep discount, in what was

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.740
intended as a form of welfare.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:03.310
Then, in the aftermath of the Asian Financial
Crisis, China opened the floodgates to everyone,

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:06.800
supported by generously low interest rates.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.250
Having taken off at the right time in the
country’s development, housing prices would

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.430
fall only once in the two decades that followed.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:20.760
But while housing gained a reputation as a
safe and profitable investment, China’s

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:25.710
stock market became synonymous with volatility
and risk.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:30.460
Because the government tightly controls how
much cash is allowed to leave the country,

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:35.930
Chinese people simply don’t have a lot of
options, and of them, housing is seen as the

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:38.410
only sure thing.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:44.330
In addition to these financial considerations,
there are simple, practical ones.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:51.930
The reality in 21st Century China is that,
for men, homeownership = marriage.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:57.060
Decades of the One Child Policy have resulted
in millions more men than women, unintentionally

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.680
tipping the scale in favor of the latter.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:05.620
Young women and their families have become
awfully picky, and that means luxury cars,

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:10.730
watches, and, yes, homes are now mere prerequisites.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:15.730
This study, for example, found that home prices
are higher in cities with more unbalanced

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.040
male-to-female ratios.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:24.100
An estimated 30% of all new home sales are
attributed to this purpose.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.140
For all of these reasons, prices have risen
to extreme levels.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:35.240
In Shenzhen, Beijing, and Shanghai, it takes
40 years of the average income to afford a

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.240
home.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.880
Today, the vast majority are bought before
construction has even started — sometimes

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.400
years in advance.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:48.190
It’s common for developers to sell batches
of property in glitzy online ‘events’,

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.860
sometimes hosted by a celebrity.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:54.360
Thousands of apartments often sell out within
minutes.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:59.290
In other cities, the right to buy new homes
is raffled-off in lotteries, giving the winners

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:04.230
mere hours to submit their down payments or
else lose their opportunity.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:09.830
What all of these insane numbers and facts
have in common is that they’re classic signs

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:12.150
of a speculative market.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:16.420
The underlying assumption is that prices will
only ever go up.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:22.490
There’s just one problem: Simple demographics
suggest otherwise.

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:30.150
In the next 10 years, China’s over-55 age
cohort will increase in size by 120 million

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.500
— the entire population of Japan.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:38.600
By the end of this century, its overall population
will be cut in half.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:45.000
The majority of homebuyers, meanwhile, are
aged between 20-50 — precisely the segment

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.530
China will soon lose.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:52.790
Not only will there be fewer people around,
but this mass of seniors will also have very

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.460
different housing needs.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:13:00.750
Ten years ago, it was easy to argue that China’s
rapid urbanization would bring more than enough

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:05.530
migrants to cities, effectively canceling
out the effects of aging.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:07.530
Today, less so.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:13.320
With an urbanization rate of 60%, the growth
rate of new migrants is clearly slowing.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:16.460
And those still arriving are getting older.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:21.350
Some are unable to find jobs, causing them
to return to the countryside.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:27.870
The uncomfortable reality is, we’ve already
seen peak migration, and it’s all downhill

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:28.920
from here.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:34.980
Eventually, it would seem, supply has to catch
up with demand.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:41.050
It doesn't take a wild imagination to draw
parallels between China today and the U.S.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:42.050
pre-2008.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:47.620
It’s time to address the elephant in the
room: What would a popping of this bubble

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:48.630
look like?

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:54.290
The simplest answer is that housing directly
contributes 15% of GDP.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:58.610
But this significantly undersells its importance.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:03.710
Economists estimate that when everything related
to real estate is accounted for — construction,

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:11.530
appliances, furniture, agents, and so on — that
number is really closer to 30%.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:16.490
Its largest banks are, unsurprisingly, heavily
dependent on mortgages.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:24.810
It’s estimated that a 20% fall in real estate
activity could lead to a 5-10% drop in GDP.

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:31.550
The IMF, meanwhile, estimates a 1% drop in
China’s demand would decrease global GDP

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:32.830
by .25%.

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:39.830
Banks, families, businesses, and the nation
as a whole have a staggering number of eggs

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.510
in one, questionable, basket.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:49.930
Now, let’s be clear: A sudden and rapid
fall in prices is the worst-case scenario.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:54.850
More than a few experts argue this is far
from inevitable, and there are credible reasons

00:14:54.850 --> 00:15:00.010
to think China is not about to experience
another 2008.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:06.030
One difference that cannot go unnoticed are
the high down payments required in China — sometimes

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.980
as high as one-third of the price.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:13.440
For second homes, 70% is not uncommon.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:19.210
These buyers are not being sold bad loans
— the government has in place truly strict

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.390
purchasing requirements.

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:25.500
People are simply going to extreme lengths
to afford them — pooling together money

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:30.890
with family, forgoing all other luxuries,
and borrowing from friends.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:34.980
Likewise, pessimism is far from new.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:40.890
Experts and non-experts alike have anticipated
a financial catastrophe for years, and they’ve

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.740
been proven wrong every single time.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:49.860
It would be a shame, however, to let extreme
hypothetical disaster overshadow the very

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:53.330
real consequences felt today.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:54.920
Forget collapse.

00:15:54.920 --> 00:16:00.020
Something truly, deeply, unhealthy is going
on already.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:05.370
Housing this unaffordable is a problem all
on its own and an illness need not be fatal

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.980
to cause significant harm.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:13.741
Remember how we got here — Chinese consumers
only have so much money, and by spending it

00:16:13.741 --> 00:16:19.840
all on empty 3rd homes, they don’t spend
it on more productive alternatives.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.580
Because banks rely on property as collateral,
companies are forced to shift their spending

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:28.800
away from innovation, manufacturing, and trade.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:34.480
If prices fall, yes, there would be serious
consequences, but if they don’t fall, regular

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:38.980
people everywhere will, quite literally, pay
the price.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:44.610
Beyond the obvious stress this generates,
high prices constrain mobility, particularly

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:49.880
for the same rural migrants China desperately
needs to keep the momentum going.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:54.930
Not to mention the millions of men left behind
with no hope of marriage.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:17:00.950
The best counter-argument goes something like
this: “Housing policy is well understood,

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:05.610
solutions to problems like these are known,
and if any government in the world has the

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:10.720
unilateral authority to implement reforms,
China is it”.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.530
When its stock markets experience too much
volatility, China simply bans those words

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.670
from social media.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:22.300
The issue with this argument is that it conflates
‘can’ with ‘will’.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:27.660
However paramount China’s central government
may appear from the outside, the reality is

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.010
much more complicated.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:35.140
Local and provincial governments, while subservient,
are not puppets.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:40.270
Politicians everywhere have their own set
of goals and interests, which may or may not

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:45.290
align with those of the nation — just as
American states don’t always see eye-to-eye

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:47.300
with D.C.

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:53.120
One reason for this divergence is that local
governments are placed in an awkward position:

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:58.940
In 1994 they were handed the responsibility
of paying for their own social services.

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:03.190
Most taxes, meanwhile, are sent straight to
Beijing.

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:09.350
Suddenly overwhelmed with bills and no way
to pay them, local authorities got clever.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:14.730
The one power they do have is control of land
— all of which is owned by the state in

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:15.730
China.

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:21.210
And so they created what are basically state-owned
shell companies called “Local Government

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.059
Financing Vehicles”.

00:18:23.059 --> 00:18:29.770
They gave these LGFVs free valuable land,
which they then used to take out loans that

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:32.630
local governments themselves couldn’t.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:37.610
The trick is that because their debt is hidden,
local governments appear far healthier than

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:43.280
they really are, while at the same time, meeting
the quotas set by Beijing.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:49.700
Following the 2008 crisis, LGFVs transformed
from a little quirk of its financial system

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:52.680
to the backbone of local economies.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:57.910
If these ‘financing vehicles’ default
on their loans, or if housing prices fall

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:02.390
too steeply, local governments now have just
as much to lose as homeowners.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:09.290
If a local government stops taking out loans,
it instantly loses over a third of its revenue,

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.220
causing a different kind of doomsday.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:17.230
So while the central government may direct
local officials to control their debt, the

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:20.530
best they can really do is feign cooperation.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:24.350
Again, these are not insurmountable challenges.

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:29.730
But the solutions are far too costly to assume
their implementation.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:34.340
Anyone who portrays the Chinese government
as an all-powerful monolith, always willing

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:40.190
to make such drastic structural reforms must
contend with its complete inaction when faced

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:45.130
with a declining birth rate during the final
years of the One-Child Policy.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:50.920
To solve the problem would require a delicate
and invasive surgery, one which risks making

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:52.950
the problem worse.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:59.260
To accept these short-term losses to its economic
growth, its social stability, and global narrative,

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:03.370
the country’s leaders would need to exhibit
wild confidence.

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:09.100
To survive such an operation, in other words,
its health would otherwise need to be quite

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:10.100
strong.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:17.060
In truth, China’s government knows it faces
several ailing organs in the years to come,

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:21.490
including one at the very heart of its continued
physical survival…

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:26.380
Next time, in Part 3, of China’s Reckoning.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:31.160
In the meantime, you might be wondering how
the housing market has fared during Coronavirus.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:37.420
When it first began spreading, alarms were
raised that perhaps this would finally initiate

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.940
the housing collapse long anticipated.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:45.740
After a rocky first few months of the year,
not only were skeptics proven wrong, but the

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:48.930
pandemic drove prices even higher.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:54.750
By June, they were up 4.9% and one of its
largest developers had increased its annual

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:57.710
sales target by 23%.

00:20:57.710 --> 00:21:02.610
How does it, time and time again, avoid disaster?

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:07.380
Before we reconvene for part 3, I’m going
to assign you some homework to answer this

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:08.380
question.

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:13.420
“China: The Bubble that Never Pops” is
one of the books I myself listened to on Audible

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:18.400
while researching this video, and consider
it essential listening to understand the whole

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:22.559
picture of what we’ve just scratched the
surface of in this video.

